---
import Landing from "../../layouts/Landing.astro";
import Footer from "../../features/navigation/components/Footer.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../features/navigation/components/SiteHeader.astro";

const locale = "en";
---

<Landing locale={locale} title="Privacy Policy — Qably" description="Which data Qably stores, which external services receive data, and how data is deleted.">
  <SiteHeader locale={locale} altHref="/privacy" />

  {/* Main Privacy Content */}
  <main id="main-content" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pt-28 pb-16 flex-grow font-sans text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed space-y-12">
    <header class="space-y-3 pb-8 border-b border-white/[0.08]">
      <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">Last updated: October 20, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="text-base text-zinc-400">
        Which data Qably stores, which external services receive data, and how data is deleted.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white tracking-tight">1. Data Qably stores</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">
        Qably stores the data it needs to show test results, document cases, and manage access:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-zinc-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>CI results</strong>: the status of each case (<code class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">pass</code>, <code class="text-rose-400 font-mono">fail</code>, <code class="text-zinc-400 font-mono">skip</code>), duration in milliseconds, suite and case names, and the error messages and details of failing tests, such as the stack trace.</li>
        <li><strong>Git context</strong>: the commit SHA, message, and author, the branch name and, for GitHub events, the title, the modified files, and the pull request reference.</li>
        <li><strong>Users and organizations</strong>: name, email address, role, organization, and authenticated session data. Invitations store the invited email address.</li>
        <li><strong>Proposal evidence</strong>: the path of the test file and a short quote of its code.</li>
        <li><strong>Conversations with Aeris</strong>: the messages and replies of each project chat thread.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white tracking-tight">2. External services involved</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">
        These are the external services that receive data from Qably or send it:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-zinc-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>GitHub</strong>: Qably reads each test file it documents from the connected repository, without cloning it, and receives the push and pull request events that GitHub sends through a webhook.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Gemini (AI)</strong>: receives the content of the test file, up to a character limit that depends on the plan. In conversations with Aeris it also receives the message, the history, the project context and, when a case is consulted, fragments of its test file.</li>
        <li><strong>Resend (email)</strong>: sends account emails, invitations, and email notifications.</li>
        <li><strong>Slack and Discord</strong>: receive notifications only in organizations that configure a webhook.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white tracking-tight">3. Credential security</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">
        Webhook secrets, repository access tokens, and Slack and Discord webhook URLs are encrypted with <strong>AES-256-GCM</strong> before they are stored. For API keys, only a <strong>SHA-256</strong> hash is stored, so they cannot be shown again. Communication with the application and the API uses HTTPS.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white tracking-tight">4. Data deletion</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">
        An owner or an admin can delete a project from the application. That action also deletes its runs, test cases, evidence, proposals, and conversations with Aeris. Each conversation with Aeris can also be deleted on its own.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer locale={locale} />
</Landing>
